## Lecture 2
    incrementality:
        a way of knowing that someone bought something because of the ad and not just a corelation
        the difference between advertising-generated conversions and the conversions that would have occurred anyway without the campaign
    key points of incrementality
    1. causal inference: its rooted in causal inference, aiming to isolate the effect of a specific action from other factors
    2. control groups: key method involves using control groups to measure what would have happened in teh absence of teh intervention
    3. lift: it is often expressed as lift, representing the increase in desired outcomes
    4. A/B testing: commonly used technique to measure incrementality include A/B testing whesre one group is exposed to the treatment adn teh other isn't
    5. attribution models: it is crucial for accurate attribution models, ensuring that credit is assigned correctly to the actions that drive the results
