# what do we measure
    ROAS (return on advertising spend)
        Rev attributed to ads/ ad spending
    iROAS: only if we have causal identification
    they aren't the same because attribution is usually correlational
# diminishing returns
    mROAS is the tangent to the sales response curve with ad spending on X and Rev on Y
    ROAS is used for overall evaluation and mROAS is used for budget reallocation
    Maxing ROI implies the highest mROAS which would occur at 0
 # Marketing Mix Model(MMM)
    consists of the 4 P's, typically uses marketing mix variables to explain sales
        when possible, MMM should include competitor variables also
        what happens to sales when price and ads increase at same time
# Using mROAS to reallocate spending
    MMM fits a sales response curve per channel
    ROAS maximization requires harmonizing mROAS across channels
        Try to get the tangents to be parallel and transfer spending from the one with the lower slope to the one with the higher slope
# Popular ad experiments
    1. Going dark: randomly withhold ads. Estimates iROAS (dropbox and ebay)
    2. Weight test: randomize spending between high and low spend treatments, mROAS
    3. Randomize messages within a campaign
    4. randomize budget across platforms, publishers, behavioral targets, contexts
    Multi Cell weight test: gives iROAS at both spending lebel and incremental mROAS between spending levels
# unified marketing measurement
    mngmnt considers MMM, experiments, attributions, and brand lift test holistically, seeking to understand relationship between them and pros/cons of each rather than giving primacy to any single measure
    when measure overlap, we cna compare them directly, to estimate the bias of less credible measure. if an experiment result is half as large as a comparable attribution, then similar attributions might be deflated by 50%. Stability of intermeasure comparisons then become important
